    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The majority highlighted that the dissent fundamentally misunderstands the difference between an unenumerated right existing under traditional due process analysis versus a fully protected fundamental right, arguing that the Court cannot simply extend rights based on general policy preferences. Specifically, Chief Justice Roberts and the majority noted that the dissent's reliance on analogies to other contexts neglects the distinct constitutional history of the unborn and fails to provide a coherent rule for when partial-birth abortion remains permissible versus when it constitutes a violation. Furthermore, the majority pointed out that the dissent's argument for restricting this constitutional right would likely create a visual upheaval in the unenumerated rights landscape, potentially reaching absurd conclusions by restricting entire non-constitutional categories of rights. Ultimately, the dissent is viewed as legally flawed because it attempts to apply abstract principles of equality and due process without respecting the specific historical limits of the amendment's text as defined by the majority.",
      "source": "target",
